2. NASA PSI table data: CC0-1.0

The test rows (data/tests.json, data/tests.csv, web/data/tests*.json, web/api/v1/tests.json,
web/api/v1/tests.csv, web/api/v1/tests/*.csv) are normalised copies of the "Experimental table"
files of NASA's Physical Sciences Informatics (PSI) system, https://psi.nasa.gov. The PSI records
state their licence as CC0-1.0 (Creative Commons Zero 1.0 Universal,
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/). They stay under CC0-1.0 here. Every row keeps
its PSI investigation id, its table file name, its row number and its raw cells, so it can be
traced back to the original.


3. NASA text: quoted with attribution

The quotes in the evidence files (seed/, config/, research/evidence/, web/data/, web/api/v1/) are
verbatim sentences from NASA web pages, NASA fact sheets and NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS)
records. The cached texts under research/raw/ are extracts of those same NASA sources, kept so that
anyone can rerun the quote check. Each quote and each cached text carries the URL of its source.
This text is NASA's, not Team Zerack's, and the MIT terms above do not relicense it. Text prepared
by NASA is generally not subject to copyright in the United States; reuse it under NASA's media
usage guidelines and credit NASA as the source.

A few short quotes come from the NASA Space Apps Challenge 2026 page for "Flame in Freefall". They
are quoted with attribution and marked as such; the page text itself is not redistributed here.

The claims, rules, decisions and status labels built on top of these quotes are VESTA's
interpretation, written with SI assistance and marked as such in the product.


4. Font

web/assets/fonts/SpaceGrotesk.woff2 is Space Grotesk, under the SIL Open Font License 1.1. Its
licence text is web/assets/fonts/OFL.txt.


VESTA is a decision-support prototype built for the NASA Space Apps Challenge 2026. It is not
certified flight software, and it is not endorsed by NASA. No NASA logo, insignia or emblem is used.
